I'm not convinced.  I've gotten to the planes with a Valk, two
weaponing Grayswandir and a long sword (both +7), and as a
Wizard, depending on a ring of combat and wands of teleporting
(with Magicbane to absorb curses).  The style of each was very
different (although I don't think I threw daggers once after
having killed Rodney---in both cases, I could cast attack spells
well enough to use them as my ranged attack, if absolutely
necessary).

    [...]
> * Thrown daggers pose the significant tactical problem of having to go and
> pick them up again.

> This last point is the most important one. Rogues gain no
> advantage while killing popcorn monsters, as all roles are
> able to kill them easily, but for tougher monsters, every turn
> counts. Moving from your advantageous position to go and pick
> up your daggers wastes turns. A pet may pick them up, wasting
> turns. A monster may pick them up, wasting turns. A monster
> may throw them back at you, perhaps saving a few turns, but
> causing yet more damage, and you still have to spend a turn to
> pick them up.

Not to mention that they can fall places you can't easily get
to, like water.  If I'm playing mainly ranged weapons, I
actually prefer bow and arrow, since I can carry about 10 times
as many arrows (and arrows are pretty plentiful if you disarm
traps).

    [...]
> Daggers are hard to use against sea monsters or when a
> peaceful monster lies behind a hostile one.

Especially if the peaceful monster is something like a
shopkeeper or a priest.

